IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ip-nouns`
IMPORT `ip-framework`
IMPORT `ip-rule-of-reason`
IMPORT `ip-restraints`
IMPORT `ip-safety-zone`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for the Licensing of Intellectual Property (2017) -- the ten
-- numbered Examples, as fixtures.
--
-- Every expected value is the answer the Example's Discussion gives, applied through the
-- body rule it rests on. The Examples are hypothetical (n.19). Where an Example reaches
-- its bottom line from considerations the body does not turn into a test (Examples 1, 7
-- and 9: "would be unlikely to object", "unlikely to challenge"), the fixture gives the
-- agencies' finding stated in the Example as an input and asserts what the body rule
-- does with it; the bottom line is not computed from the facts. Where the body's modal
-- differs from the Example's (3.4 "will challenge the restraint under the per se rule";
-- Example 6 "would be likely to challenge the arrangement under the per se rule"), the
-- result keeps the body's
-- and the comment says so.
--
-- Not independent: the technology-market competitor test (3.2.2) and the blocking rule
-- (2.3) were read with Examples 4, 5 and 9 in view, so those fixtures confirm a reading
-- partly taken from them. NOTES.md sections 5 and 6 record each Example's disposition.
--
-- A failing assertion is a finding: no expected value was edited to match the code.

§ `The Examples`

§§ `Fixtures shared by the Examples`

-- A restraint that is not of a per se type, can be expected to contribute to an
-- efficiency-enhancing integration, and is reasonably related to efficiencies.
`an integrative restraint` MEANS `Restraint in a licensing arrangement` WITH
    `the type of restraint` IS `a type of restraint not accorded per se treatment`
    `the restraint is of a kind that would always or almost always tend to reduce output or increase prices` IS FALSE
    `the restraint can be expected to contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration of economic activity` IS TRUE
    `the restraint is reasonably related to efficiencies` IS TRUE

-- The agencies' finding that a restraint is unlikely to have an anticompetitive effect.
`the finding of no likely anticompetitive effect` MEANS `Findings under the rule of reason` WITH
    `the agencies conclude that the restraint is unlikely to have an anticompetitive effect` IS TRUE
    `it is clear that the parties could have achieved similar efficiencies by practical and significantly less restrictive means` IS FALSE
    `the restraint is reasonably necessary to achieve procompetitive efficiencies` IS FALSE
    `the duration of the restraint clearly exceeds the period needed to achieve the procompetitive efficiency` IS FALSE

-- No finding yet beyond the facts of the restraint.
`no findings yet` MEANS `Findings under the rule of reason` WITH
    `the agencies conclude that the restraint is unlikely to have an anticompetitive effect` IS FALSE
    `it is clear that the parties could have achieved similar efficiencies by practical and significantly less restrictive means` IS FALSE
    `the restraint is reasonably necessary to achieve procompetitive efficiencies` IS FALSE
    `the duration of the restraint clearly exceeds the period needed to achieve the procompetitive efficiency` IS FALSE

GIVEN m IS A STRING
      actual IS A BOOLEAN
      potential IS A BOOLEAN
      complementary IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Two parties in a relevant market (3.3)`
`two parties in` MEANS `Two parties in a relevant market (3.3)` WITH
    `the market` IS m
    `they would have been actual competitors in the market in the absence of the license` IS actual
    `it is reasonably probable that one would have become a competitor of the other in the market in the absence of the licensing arrangement` IS potential
    `the arrangement affects activities of theirs in the market that are in a complementary relationship` IS complementary

GIVEN substitute IS A BOOLEAN
      blocked IS A BOOLEAN
      licensesOut IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Two parties' technologies (3.2.2)`
`technologies where` MEANS `Two parties' technologies (3.2.2)` WITH
    `the other party controls a technology or good close enough a substitute to constrain significantly the exercise of market power with respect to the licensed technology` IS substitute
    `one party's technology cannot be practiced without using the other's` IS blocked
    `either party licenses its technology to others` IS licensesOut

§§ `Example 1 -- ComputerCo [pdf pages 8-9]`

-- Situation: a copyrighted inventory program licensed with field-of-use limits
-- (hospitals; group medical practices) and territorial limits, at different royalties;
-- nothing stops licensees from developing, using or selling any other program; no
-- licensee is an actual or potential competitor of ComputerCo in inventory programs.
-- Discussion: no likely harm to competition among actual or potential competitors, so the
-- agency "would be unlikely to object"; the conclusion "could differ if" the license barred
-- licensees from using any other such program. n.22: the same for a patent or a trade
-- secret.

#ASSERT `1.0: whether the guidelines cover licensing of` `a copyright` EQUALS `covered by these guidelines`
#ASSERT `1.0: whether the guidelines cover licensing of` `a patent` EQUALS `covered by these guidelines`
#ASSERT `1.0: whether the guidelines cover licensing of` `a trade secret` EQUALS `covered by these guidelines`

-- ComputerCo and a hospital licensee in the market for inventory management programs:
-- not competitors; the license combines the program with the licensee's complementary
-- factors of production.
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "inventory management programs" FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `a vertical relationship`

-- No way of harming competition among actual or potential competitors appears, so 3.1
-- states nothing; the Example's conclusion is the agencies' finding, which 3.4 and 4.2
-- then turn into "will treat it as reasonable" and "will not challenge".
#ASSERT `3.1: when competition among actual or potential competitors may be adversely affected` EMPTY EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `3.4: the mode of analysis` `an integrative restraint` EQUALS `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`
#ASSERT `3.4: what the agencies do with the restraint` `an integrative restraint` `the finding of no likely anticompetitive effect` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will treat the restraint as reasonable, without an elaborate analysis of market power or the justifications for it`
#ASSERT `4.2: what the agencies do on their findings` `the finding of no likely anticompetitive effect` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will not challenge the restraint`

-- The variant: a license that bars licensees from using any other inventory management
-- program forecloses access to competing technologies (3.1), and is exclusive dealing
-- (4.1.2), evaluated under the rule of reason (5.4).
`Example 1 variant: the license bars any other program` MEANS `License terms and structure (4.1.2)` WITH
    `the license by its terms is exclusive or partially exclusive, limiting the licensor's ability to license others` IS FALSE
    `the license is structured so that the licensor is unlikely to license others or to practice the technology itself` IS FALSE
    `a term of the license prevents or restrains the licensee from licensing, selling, distributing or using competing technologies` IS TRUE
    `the license is structured to increase significantly the licensee's cost when it uses competing technologies` IS FALSE
    `the license contains another restraint on the competitive conduct of the licensor or the licensee` IS TRUE
    `a party chooses to deal with a single licensor or licensee` IS FALSE
    `a party confines its activity to a single field of use or location` IS TRUE
    `only a single licensee has chosen to take a license` IS FALSE
    `some or all of the potential licensees choose to use the licensed technology to the exclusion of other technologies` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `3.1: when competition among actual or potential competitors may be adversely affected` (LIST `anticompetitively foreclosing access to, or significantly raising the price of, an important input`) EQUALS JUST `the agencies will follow the analysis set forth in sections 3.2 to 5`
#ASSERT `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive dealing` `Example 1 variant: the license bars any other program`
#ASSERT `5.4: exclusive dealing` `Example 1 variant: the license bars any other program` EQUALS JUST `exclusive dealing arrangements are evaluated under the rule of reason`

§§ `Example 2 -- Alpha and Beta [pdf pages 11-13]`

-- Situation: two firms' different patented processes for the same off-patent drug, to be
-- assigned to a joint manufacturing venture before either firm uses or licenses them;
-- the drug's manufacture and distribution is competitive. Discussion: the agency "would
-- delineate a technology market"; 3.2.2 says "may analyze", and the Example's "would" is
-- the agency's use of that permission.
-- CONSTRUCTED: the Example does not say the processes are marketed apart from the drug
-- (it is implied by their being licensable to the venture), and it gives no share data
-- and no count of technologies; those three fields are placeholders that the assertion
-- below does not read. The equal share of the Discussion is tested in
-- ip-tests-boundaries.l4.
`Example 2: the process technologies` MEANS `Technology-market facts (3.2.2)` WITH
    `the rights to the intellectual property are marketed separately from the products in which they are used` IS TRUE
    `market share data or other indicia of market power are available` IS TRUE
    `the competing technologies appear comparably efficient` IS FALSE
    `the number of technologies in the market, the licensed technology included` IS 2

#ASSERT `3.2.2: whether a technology market is analysed` `Example 2: the process technologies` EQUALS `the agencies may analyse the competitive effects of the arrangement in a technology market`

§§ `Example 3 -- a research and development joint venture [pdf pages 14-16]`

-- Situation: three of the largest producers of a plastic plan joint research and
-- development of a rapidly biodegradable plastic, licensing the results to the partners
-- only. Discussion: the agency would identify the firms able and minded to do closely
-- substitutable research and development, then count them (four or more; fewer).

#ASSERT `3.2.3: whether a research and development market is analysed` (`Research and development facts (3.2.3)` OF TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, 7) EQUALS JUST `the agencies may analyse the impact as a competitive effect in a separate research and development market`

GIVEN name IS A STRING
GIVETH AN `Other research and development entity`
`an independent capable entity` MEANS `Other research and development entity` OF name, name, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE

`four other entities` MEANS LIST (`an independent capable entity` "D"), (`an independent capable entity` "E"), (`an independent capable entity` "F"), (`an independent capable entity` "G")
`three other entities` MEANS LIST (`an independent capable entity` "D"), (`an independent capable entity` "E"), (`an independent capable entity` "F")

#ASSERT `Example 3: the screen for a research and development joint venture` `four other entities` NOTHING EQUALS JUST `the joint venture ordinarily would be unlikely to adversely affect competition in the relevant research and development market`
#ASSERT `Example 3: the screen for a research and development joint venture` `three other entities` NOTHING EQUALS JUST `the agency would consider whether the joint venture would give the parties an incentive and ability collectively to reduce investment in, or otherwise to retard the pace or scope of, research and development`
#ASSERT `Example 3: the screen for a research and development joint venture` `three other entities` (JUST TRUE) EQUALS JUST `the agency would proceed to consider efficiency justifications for the venture`

§§ `Example 4 -- AgCo licenses emission control technology to FarmCo [pdf page 17]`

-- Situation: AgCo's patented emission control technology is far superior; FarmCo's own
-- does not significantly constrain AgCo's prices; FarmCo cannot develop an improved
-- technology without infringing. Discussion: in the superior emission control technology
-- the relationship "is vertical"; assuming they compete in farm equipment, "their
-- relationship is horizontal" there.

-- FarmCo's technology is not a close substitute; it is not blocked by AgCo's either (it
-- exists and is inferior).
#ASSERT `3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` (`technologies where` FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not a potential competitor (n.27): any improved technology FarmCo could develop would
-- infringe. FarmCo is a consumer of AgCo's technology.
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "superior emission control technology" FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `a vertical relationship`
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "farm equipment" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`

§§ `Example 5 -- AgCo and FarmCo cross-license [pdf page 18]`

-- Situation: as Example 4, and FarmCo licenses its new valve technology to AgCo, which
-- already owns an alternative valve technology giving similar performance at comparable
-- cost and licenses it to others. Discussion: vertical for emission control; in engine
-- valve technology "vertical in part and horizontal in part"; whether they license their
-- valve technologies to others "is not important", and the relationship is horizontal
-- even if AgCo's use were captive; horizontal in farm equipment.

#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "superior emission control technology" FALSE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `a vertical relationship`
#ASSERT `3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` (`technologies where` TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The captive variant: licensing to others is not read.
#ASSERT `3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` (`technologies where` TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "engine valve technology" TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `a relationship horizontal in part and vertical in part`
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "farm equipment" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`

§§ `Example 6 -- Gamma's sham process license [pdf pages 20-21]`

-- Situation: Gamma licenses a process that is no economic improvement to every other
-- manufacturer of Product X; none uses it; each gets an exclusive territory and no
-- manufacturer may sell X, however made, outside it. Discussion: horizontal in the goods
-- market for X and in the technology market; the licensees are "formally in a vertical
-- relationship" with Gamma, which is not significant; no useful transfer of technology,
-- so no efficiency-enhancing integration; the agency "would be likely to challenge the
-- arrangement under the per se rule". 3.4's own words for the case are "will challenge the
-- restraint under the per se rule"; the result keeps 3.4's.

`Example 6: the territorial allocation` MEANS `Restraint in a licensing arrangement` WITH
    `the type of restraint` IS `market division among horizontal competitors`
    `the restraint is of a kind that would always or almost always tend to reduce output or increase prices` IS FALSE   -- not stated; immaterial to the per se mode
    `the restraint can be expected to contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration of economic activity` IS FALSE
    `the restraint is reasonably related to efficiencies` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "Product X" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship has a horizontal component` (`two parties in` "technology to manufacture Product X" TRUE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT `3.4: the mode of analysis` `Example 6: the territorial allocation` EQUALS `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
#ASSERT `3.4: what the agencies do with the restraint` `Example 6: the territorial allocation` `no findings yet` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule, without an elaborate inquiry into its likely competitive effect`

-- Were the technology an advance that the licensees used, contributing to an
-- efficiency-enhancing integration, the agency "would analyze the arrangement under the
-- rule of reason".
`Example 6 variant: an advance the licensees use` MEANS `Restraint in a licensing arrangement` WITH
    `the type of restraint` IS `market division among horizontal competitors`
    `the restraint is of a kind that would always or almost always tend to reduce output or increase prices` IS FALSE
    `the restraint can be expected to contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration of economic activity` IS TRUE
    `the restraint is reasonably related to efficiencies` IS TRUE

#ASSERT `3.4: the mode of analysis` `Example 6 variant: an advance the licensees use` EQUALS `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`

-- The Example adds that territorial and field-of-use allocation are generally alike: both
-- are market division, one type. And the form of the property does not matter.
#ASSERT `1.0: whether the guidelines cover licensing of` `know-how` EQUALS `covered by these guidelines`

-- The safety zone: market division is facially anticompetitive (n.59), whatever the
-- shares. (A consequence of the body rules for Example 6's restraint, not the Example's
-- own statement.)
#ASSERT `4.3: the restraint is facially anticompetitive` `Example 6: the territorial allocation`

§§ `Example 7 -- NewCo's exclusive license to BigCo [pdf pages 24-25]`

-- Situation: NewCo grants BigCo an exclusive license to sell a product embodying its new
-- display technology, with a term barring BigCo from selling competing products; they are
-- not actual or potential competitors; the market is unconcentrated, BigCo is a small
-- proportion of the outlets, entry is easy; demand is uncertain. Discussion: the exclusive
-- license does not affect competition between licensor and licensee; the exclusive
-- dealing is unlikely to harm competition, the market being unconcentrated, the outlets
-- affected few and entry easy; so the agency "would be unlikely to challenge the
-- arrangement".

`Example 7: the license` MEANS `License terms and structure (4.1.2)` WITH
    `the license by its terms is exclusive or partially exclusive, limiting the licensor's ability to license others` IS TRUE
    `the license is structured so that the licensor is unlikely to license others or to practice the technology itself` IS FALSE
    `a term of the license prevents or restrains the licensee from licensing, selling, distributing or using competing technologies` IS TRUE
    `the license is structured to increase significantly the licensee's cost when it uses competing technologies` IS FALSE
    `the license contains another restraint on the competitive conduct of the licensor or the licensee` IS FALSE
    `a party chooses to deal with a single licensor or licensee` IS TRUE
    `a party confines its activity to a single field of use or location` IS FALSE
    `only a single licensee has chosen to take a license` IS FALSE
    `some or all of the potential licensees choose to use the licensed technology to the exclusion of other technologies` IS FALSE

`Example 7: no horizontal relationship` MEANS `Horizontal relationships among the parties (4.1.2)` OF FALSE, FALSE, FALSE

`Example 7: the display market` MEANS `Market characteristics (4.1.1, 5.4)` WITH
    `the relevant markets are concentrated` IS FALSE
    `entry into the relevant markets is difficult` IS FALSE
    `supply and demand respond little to changes in price` IS FALSE
    `the restraint affects a large proportion of the relevant markets` IS FALSE
    `the restraint is of long duration` IS FALSE
    `owners of competing technologies impose similar restraints on their licensees` IS FALSE
    `competing licensees are subject to common restraints from a common licensor or competing licensors` IS FALSE

-- The exclusive license may encourage BigCo to develop and promote the product; the
-- Example finds no anticompetitive foreclosure.
`Example 7: the exclusive dealing` MEANS `Exclusive dealing facts (5.4)` WITH
    `the arrangement promotes the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology` IS TRUE
    `the arrangement anticompetitively forecloses the exploitation and development of, or otherwise constrains competition among, competing technologies` IS FALSE
    `the restraint encourages licensees to develop and market the licensed technology or specialized applications of it` IS TRUE
    `the restraint increases licensors' incentives to develop or refine the licensed technology` IS FALSE
    `the restraint otherwise increases competition and enhances output in a relevant market` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive licensing` `Example 7: the license`
#ASSERT `4.1.2: what the agencies say of the license's exclusivity` `Example 7: the license` `Example 7: no horizontal relationship` EQUALS JUST `generally, the exclusive licensing raises no antitrust concern: there is no horizontal relationship among licensors, among licensees, or between the licensor and a licensee`
#ASSERT `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive dealing` `Example 7: the license`
#ASSERT `5.4: exclusive dealing` `Example 7: the license` EQUALS JUST `exclusive dealing arrangements are evaluated under the rule of reason`
#ASSERT `4.1.1: the considerations bearing on the risk of harm that are present` `Example 7: the display market` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `5.4: the matters present` `Example 7: the exclusive dealing` EQUALS LIST `promotion of the exploitation and development of the licensor's technology`, `encouragement of licensees to develop and market the licensed technology or its specialized applications`
-- The bottom line, from the Example's finding (not computed from the factors above).
-- Example: "would be unlikely to challenge"; 4.2: "will not challenge".
#ASSERT `4.2: what the agencies do on their findings` `the finding of no likely anticompetitive effect` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will not challenge the restraint`

§§ `Example 8 -- jointly assigned substitute patents [pdf pages 28-29]`

-- Situation: two leading manufacturers assign patents on alternative, non-blocking,
-- substitutable circuit designs to a jointly owned corporation that licenses them and sets
-- the royalties. Discussion: horizontal in the goods market and the technology markets;
-- without efficiency-enhancing integration the agency may conclude the joint marketing
-- "constitutes horizontal price-fixing", open to per se challenge; with integration, the
-- rule of reason; but effects "sufficiently apparent" and efficiencies weak or unrelated
-- may warrant challenge without an elaborate analysis. The "may conclude" is the
-- classification of the joint royalty-setting as price-fixing; once so classified, 3.4
-- says "will".

#ASSERT `3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` (`technologies where` TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "circuit design technology" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "the consumer electronic product" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`

GIVEN integration IS A BOOLEAN
      apparent IS A BOOLEAN
      related IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Restraint in a licensing arrangement`
`Example 8: the joint royalty-setting, with` MEANS `Restraint in a licensing arrangement` WITH
    `the type of restraint` IS `naked price-fixing`
    `the restraint is of a kind that would always or almost always tend to reduce output or increase prices` IS apparent
    `the restraint can be expected to contribute to an efficiency-enhancing integration of economic activity` IS integration
    `the restraint is reasonably related to efficiencies` IS related

#ASSERT `5.1: the mode of analysis of a horizontal restraint` (`Example 8: the joint royalty-setting, with` FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
#ASSERT `5.5: the mode of analysis of a collective restraint in a pool or cross-license` (`Example 8: the joint royalty-setting, with` FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `the agencies will challenge the restraint under the per se rule`
#ASSERT `5.1: the mode of analysis of a horizontal restraint` (`Example 8: the joint royalty-setting, with` TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`
-- "sufficiently apparent" effects read as 3.4's facial appearance, "weak or not reasonably
-- related" efficiencies as not reasonably related (fork F11).
#ASSERT `3.4: what the agencies do with the restraint` (`Example 8: the joint royalty-setting, with` TRUE TRUE FALSE) `no findings yet` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will likely challenge the restraint without an elaborate analysis of particular industry circumstances`

§§ `Example 9 -- jointly assigned blocking patents [pdf page 34]`

-- Situation: as Example 8, but only blocking patents are assigned: none can be used
-- without infringing a patent of the other firm. Discussion: the patents being blocking,
-- the manufacturers "are not in a horizontal relationship with respect to those patents";
-- they are horizontal in the goods market; absent collateral restraints the agency "would
-- be unlikely to challenge this arrangement".

#ASSERT `3.2.2: the parties are actual competitors in the technology market` (`technologies where` FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `3.3: the relationship has a horizontal component` (`two parties in` "the circuit designs covered by the blocking patents" FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "the consumer electronic product" TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`
#ASSERT `3.4: the mode of analysis` `an integrative restraint` EQUALS `the agencies will apply a rule of reason analysis`
-- The bottom line, from the Example's finding (not computed).
#ASSERT `4.2: what the agencies do on their findings` `the finding of no likely anticompetitive effect` EQUALS JUST `the agencies will not challenge the restraint`

§§ `Example 10 -- Omega's nominally nonexclusive license to Delta [pdf pages 36-37]`

-- Situation: Omega's patented drug, nearly approved, would be a significant competitor to
-- Delta's, the only approved drug; Omega licenses Delta instead of entering, nominally
-- nonexclusively, and rejects every reasonable request by other firms for a license.
-- Discussion: the license is "exclusive in fact"; Omega or its licensee would be a
-- potential competitor of Delta, so they are horizontal in the goods market; the agency
-- "would apply a merger analysis to this transaction, since it involves an acquisition of
-- a potential competitor". n.85 cites this Example for a license that in substance
-- transfers the rights. ASSUMED, NOT RULED (fork F8): the fixture reads the license as
-- 5.7's exclusive license in fact, so 5.7's "will apply" answers; the Example's own reason
-- is the acquisition of a potential competitor, which no rule here computes.

`Example 10: the license to Delta` MEANS `Transfer of intellectual property rights (5.7)` WITH
    `the transfer is an outright sale by the owner of all of its rights to the intellectual property` IS FALSE
    `the transferee obtains a license that in fact precludes all other persons, including the licensor, from using the licensed intellectual property` IS TRUE
    `the license in substance transfers intellectual property rights, and its effect may be to substantially lessen competition in a relevant market` IS TRUE

#ASSERT `5.7: merger analysis of the transfer` `Example 10: the license to Delta` EQUALS `the agencies will apply a merger analysis: an exclusive license`
#ASSERT `3.3: the relationship of the parties in the market` (`two parties in` "drugs for the treatment of the disease" FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS `a horizontal relationship`

-- The safety zone does not apply to a transfer to which a merger analysis is applied
-- (4.3, n.86).
`Example 10: the arrangement before the safety zone` MEANS `Licensing arrangement before the safety zone (4.3)` WITH
    `the form of the intellectual property licensed` IS `a patent`
    `the restraint is in a licensing arrangement` IS TRUE
    `the restraint is related to the use of the licensed intellectual property` IS TRUE
    `the transfer` IS `Example 10: the license to Delta`
    `the restraint` IS `an integrative restraint`
    `the analysis of goods markets alone would inadequately address the effects of the arrangement on competition among technologies or in research and development` IS FALSE
    `the relevant markets significantly affected by the restraint` IS EMPTY

#ASSERT `4.3: the antitrust safety zone` `Example 10: the arrangement before the safety zone` EQUALS JUST (`the safety zone does not apply` `the agencies apply a merger analysis to the transfer`)
